# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-will-antecedent.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2 has "wills → will_cause"; aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2.3 have "wills → will_cause" and "will_cause → nec_by_cause"; they differ when only "God wills the thing" and "the thing is related to the divine will as to an antecedent cause" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-will-antecedent.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "God wills the thing" and "the thing is related to the divine will as to an antecedent cause" are true and "the thing is necessary by its antecedent cause" is false. Is S3, "Now things created by God are related to the divine will as to an antecedent cause, whereby they have necessity.", true then?
aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2 has "will_cause ∧ □(wills → comes) → nec_by_cause"; aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the thing is related to the divine will as to an antecedent cause" and "necessarily, (if God wills the thing, then the thing comes to pass)" are true, which the second allows
